And this actually happened.

50
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.600
So I've been working since 2023

51
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.120
on making it possible to run agents

52
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.600
and LMS and Rag and all that stuff around it

53
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.800
that that runs entirely on your own infrastructure.

54
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.720
And we've been working with, with people who who care about that.

55
00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.040
And so kind of late last year, I got really obsessed with this idea

56
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.840
of making the snake eats its own tail

57
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.360
by actually using our own stuff that we were building to build itself.

58
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.800
And, and so we, we also were lucky to have customers who,

59
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.840
who wanted to push us towards the sort of coding

60
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.840
agent side of, of the equation.

61
00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.200
And so we've spent a lot of time

62
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.920
thinking about kind of this design space for systems

63
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.440
that run agents and in particular systems that, that run agents on the server.

64
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.600
So all I want to say here is

65
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.680
and we'll go through each of these in turn in the slides.

66
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.160
Kind of warning contains opinions.

67
00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.400
And the reason I'm sharing these opinions is that I hope

68
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.880
they will be helpful to anyone else who's building systems like this.

69
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.480
So kind of both lay out kind of the shape of the landscape,

70
00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.960
and then also carve out some specific opinions,

71
00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:01.840
but also why I came to have them, because you might have different opinions

72
00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.880
or you might not have thought about some of these things. So

73
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.680
the first big

74
00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.880
decision in my opinion, is do you want to carry on

75
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.840
letting every developer run whatever agent they like, or maybe the

76
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.400
the same agent that everyone uses in the company,

77
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.960
but on their own specific development environment?

78
00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.720
That's kind of like a snowflake.

79
00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:31.600
Or do you want to create a pool of

80
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.920
agents that run on your organization's infrastructure

81
00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.960
that many humans can interact with?

82
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.560
And my strong argument is that.

83
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.760
Going for the centralized approach and I say centralized,

84
00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.080
it doesn't mean you have to

85
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.800
hand over all your data to OpenAI or run on their infrastructure or anything.

86
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.720
You can run it on your own infrastructure.

87
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.160
Still on Kubernetes or something, but there are

88
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.320
some significant benefits from having a centralized approach.

89
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.560
For example, you can have a globally distributed team

90
00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.800
all around the world, and you can say

91
00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.800
that when the sun sets in Tokyo and rises in London,

92
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.240
whoever a different human can carry on doing the work

93
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.600
that a certain agent was working on, especially if that agent truly

94
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.600
has its own computer for that specific task.

95
00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.600
And so there's a really nice

96
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.760
article written by by someone.

97
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.760
We've been working with a company called DevIcon,

98
00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.840
and what they said was, to be honest, agents, coding is not new.

99
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.920
Going from doing it the easier way, which is the way on the left

100
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.960
which is unsafe and per engineer to something global per company

101
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.640
and safe is is what they found interesting in this approach.

102
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.120
So that's kind of my argument.

103
00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.000
My opinion number one is

104
00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:57.800
you should

105
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:01.080
give each agent their own computer, not each human.

106
00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.440
It's almost like you wouldn't hire

107
00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.680
a team of software developers and ask them all to share one computer.

108
00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.720
You should give each agent their own computer.

109
00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.440
The next opinion that I've come to is do.

110
00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.360
Do we still need an ID?

111
00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.200
Claude code started making us, like, not look at the code so much,

112
00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.080
and I genuinely feel like it made me stupider.

113
00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.040
My opinion is that you should still have an ID.

114
00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.080
These agents will increasingly do

115
00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.120
more autonomous, long running tasks.

116
00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.800
But when it comes to actually stepping in and pair

117
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.520
programing with an agent, you do still need to be able

118
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.120
to look at the code, and having a visual display

119
00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.240
that follows what the agent is doing is actually really useful

120
00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.360
when you want to understand what it's doing.

121
00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.280
Another thing that really matters is that things have to be fast.

122
00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:01.400
I got incredibly frustrated with with cursor were back when I was a cursor user,

123
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.520
and just how slow it was at Hadley keyboard input.

124
00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.520
And then Claude code came out with anthropic, came up with Claude code.

125
00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.400
And they just do you know Claude code is implemented in react.

126
00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:16.240
And so they've ended up having to implement

127
00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:17.880
basically a web browser in the terminal.

128
00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:21.600
And it's just like, can't we just have a working text box?

129
00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:26.000
So anyway, the next thing I want to talk about is

130
00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.520
how you organize your agents.

131
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.840
And there's kind of two different schools of thought here.

132
00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.240
The first one is do you scale your agents out by number of tasks,

133
00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:43.960
or do you try and build like an organization out of your agents,

134
00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.440
and you hire a CEO agent

135
00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.440
and they hire a VP of engineering agent, and the VP of engineering

136
00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.440
agent hires some engineers and you give them all names or something.

137
00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.960
And we are.

138
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.720
We started with the left hand approach because it's easier, but

139
00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.680
we're researching the right hand approach.

140
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.480
But so far what we've researched, what we've found in the research

141
00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.600
is if you go to fine grained on giving each agent their own

142
00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.760
specific role and you give them these special channels they can communicate on,

143
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:15.080
like Agent Slack or something, they end up

144
00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.080
kind of devolving into enterprise politics.

145
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:22.600
Because they were trained on

146
00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:27.200
all of this human data of how humans argue with each other about stupid stuff.

147
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.760
So you end up burning a lot of tokens like that.

148
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.120
But what I, what I actually think is ultimately going to be appropriate

149
00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.800
here is to combine the models where the

150
00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:42.640
you have these coarse grained, different categorizations of roles

151
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.440
like marketing agents and sales agents and engineering agents,

152
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.880
because they all need different tools and connectivity to different systems.

153
00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.520
But within those you scale the tasks, scale the agents by task.

154
00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:00.280
So you have a sort of pool of of bees maybe, and so on.

155
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.960
So yeah, I also wanted to share some kind of demos

156
00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:10.840
as I go to to illustrate the design decisions I'm talking about.

157
00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:13.120
So I'll start with this one.

158
00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.800
So what we put together here,

159
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.600
this is basically this idea of scaling by by task.

160
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.600
And the notion there is that

161
00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.680
Kanban is quite a natural fit for scaling by task, because you want to

162
00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.600
constrain the parallelism. So you can kick off.

163
00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.200
This is a sample project that has a to do list app.

