00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.080
But basically what's happening here from a process perspective

188
00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.840
is the agent has.

189
00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:01.160
Started up inside the IDE.

190
00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.480
We use Zed because it's fast.

191
00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.360
And we also forked it so that you can remote control it.

192
00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.280
So we can inject prompts into the Zed agent.

193
00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:14.720
And the other thing that's nice about Z is that it supports ACP,

194
00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:19.240
which means it integrates with all of the major agent harnesses.

195
00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.280
So it works with Codex and Claude code.

196
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:22.960
We're not trying to reinvent that bill

197
00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.960
then.

198
00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:26.320
Yeah.

199
00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.800
So the next thing is like, well, what's that agent actually going to do

200
00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:32.120
when it starts up?

201
00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.840
And this is a Tessl conference.

202
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.280
So it would be bad not to talk about spec-driven development,

203
00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.400
but I'm actually a huge fan of spec driven development.

204
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:41.640
So the approach that we've taken is.

205
00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:47.560
Spec is central to to what's going on.

206
00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:51.120
The the human prompt

207
00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.320
that comes in is something really short, like add dark mode

208
00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.920
support to my to do list app or something kind of really trivial.

209
00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:03.160
And that prompt goes into the into this agent.

210
00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:04.640
It's actually the same agent.

211
00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:08.600
It's just the agent has an explicit planning phase and later

212
00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.040
in implementation phase.

213
00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:14.120
So we prompt the agent to say please write a plan.

214
00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.240
Because it turns out that

215
00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:19.840
if you get the agent to write a plan before it does the work, you get much,

216
00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.880
much better results because you can then correct it and you can kind of

217
00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.480
do that design work before the agent has gone off

218
00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.560
in the wrong direction, when it's harder to steer it,

219
00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.520
if it's in the middle of doing an implementation

220
00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.520
and it's got it's misunderstood, something fundamental.

221
00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:36.240
So you put the prompt in into the planning phase,

222
00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:40.080
and then you have a human in the loop commenting, which you'll see in a second.

223
00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.600
And then but, but the spec is the combination

224
00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:47.280
of the human prompt and the planning agent reading the code.

225
00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.800
So I don't know if you can see this red arrow here.

226
00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:50.920
We've got a code

227
00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.280
check out for the project, which is whatever repos you've attached.

228
00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:59.400
And the job of the planning phase is based on the user's requirement.

229
00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.480
Write a good spec based on also looking at the code,

230
00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.880
so that you understand what you're going to do

231
00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.080
in quite a lot more detail than the user specified.

232
00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:13.080
And then once you approve the spec.

233
00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.160
So there's a human human in the loop.

234
00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.480
On commenting on the spec,

235
00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:18.720
there's also a human in the loop on approving spec

236
00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.840
to say, yes, I'm happy with that spec.

237
00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:25.720
Then you go into the implementation phase and because each agent

238
00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.920
has its own desktop environment, you can now do some interesting things

239
00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:32.040
like the agent can self if it's a web application.

240
00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.520
And lots and lots of things are web applications.

241
00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:38.640
So you can then have the agent put up a PR on GitHub,

242
00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:42.840
but then also kind of human in the loop review both the PR

243
00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:47.840
but also the running application in that agents own computer.

244
00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:51.720
And because each agent has its own isolated computer, they're actually

245
00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.920
Docker and Docker environments or Docker on Kubernetes environments.

246
00:13:54.920 --> 00:14:00.400
They can they're not treading on each other's toes when

247
00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:06.320
they're bringing up a Chrome instance and using Chrome MCP to navigate around.

248
00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.520
So yeah, I'll show you this.

249
00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:15.400
Spec review phase.

250
00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:20.960
So here we had these three tasks or four tasks.

